title: A traffic light: your first state machine
mode: vhdl
chapter: Counting
check: behaves
cycles: 120
random: rst=0.05
---- intro
A traffic light goes red, green, amber, red ... Each colour is a **state**, and at each clock edge the light moves to the next one. A circuit like this is a **state machine**.

VHDL lets you give the states names. Declare a type with one name per state, and a signal of that type (both are in the starter):

```
type state_t is (s_red, s_green, s_amber);
signal state : state_t := s_red;
```

A `case` inside the clock edge says where each state goes next, and the outputs say which light is on in each state.
---- task
Step the light red → green → amber → red at each rising edge; `rst` goes back to red. Light the output for the current state.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity traffic is
  port(
    clk   : in  std_logic;
    rst   : in  std_logic;
    red   : out std_logic;
    amber : out std_logic;
    green : out std_logic
  );
end traffic;

architecture rtl of traffic is
    type state_t is (s_red, s_green, s_amber);
    signal state : state_t := s_red;
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity traffic is
  port(
    clk   : in  std_logic;
    rst   : in  std_logic;
    red   : out std_logic;
    amber : out std_logic;
    green : out std_logic
  );
end traffic;

architecture rtl of traffic is
    type state_t is (s_red, s_green, s_amber);
    signal state : state_t := s_red;
begin
    process (clk)
    begin
        if rising_edge(clk) then
            if rst = '1' then
                state <= s_red;
            else
                case state is
                    when s_red   => state <= s_green;
                    when s_green => state <= s_amber;
                    when s_amber => state <= s_red;
                end case;
            end if;
        end if;
    end process;
    red   <= '1' when state = s_red   else '0';
    amber <= '1' when state = s_amber else '0';
    green <= '1' when state = s_green else '0';
end rtl;
---- hint1
Inside the clock edge: the reset, then `case state is when s_red => state <= s_green; ... end case;`.
---- hint2
After the process, one line per light: `red <= '1' when state = s_red else '0';` and the same for `amber` and `green`.
---- solution
```
process (clk)
begin
    if rising_edge(clk) then
        if rst = '1' then
            state <= s_red;
        else
            case state is
                when s_red   => state <= s_green;
                when s_green => state <= s_amber;
                when s_amber => state <= s_red;
            end case;
        end if;
    end if;
end process;
red   <= '1' when state = s_red   else '0';
amber <= '1' when state = s_amber else '0';
green <= '1' when state = s_green else '0';
```
---- done
You wrote a state machine: name the states, say where each one goes, say what each one shows. Vending machines, network chips and the control unit of a processor are all built this way. From one line, `y <= a;`, to here.
